{{also|Raamattu}}
==Finnish==

===Etymology===
Borrowed from {{bor|fi|orv|грамота}}, from {{der|fi|grc|γράμματα|t=letters, writing}}. <ref>{{R:Hakkinen 2004}}</ref> According to Häkkinen (2018), the word was borrowed through {{bor|fi|et|raamat}},<ref>Häkkinen, Kaisa. ''Suomi on kuuden kerroksen kieli'' (2018). ''Tiede''.</ref> to which it is at least definitely related. If the word is borrowed from Estonian, it likely originally simply meant "book", and some evidence for this can be found (such as how Agricola uses {{m|fi|raamattu}} for the books of Moses); in this case, the narrower sense for the Bible specifically developed through ellipsis of the name "{{m|fi||pyhä Raamattu}}", originally literally "holy book", and later "Holy Bible".

